# TryHackMe — Nmap In-Depth

Reference notes on Nmap switches, scan types, and scripting. This is a flags/concepts cheatsheet, not an investigation walkthrough.

---

## 1. Essential Switches & Flags

| Flag | What it does |
|---|---|
| `-sS` | TCP SYN scan ("stealth"/"half-open" scan). Default when run as root/sudo. |
| `-sT` | TCP Connect scan. Full three-way handshake. Used when you don't have raw packet privileges. |
| `-sU` | UDP scan. Slower — UDP is stateless, so Nmap infers state from silence or ICMP "destination unreachable" errors. |
| `-Pn` | Skip host discovery entirely; treat all targets as up. |
| `-p` | Port targeting. `-p 80` (single), `-p 1000-1500` (range), `-p-` (all 65,535 ports). |
| `-A` | Aggressive mode — bundles OS detection, version detection, script scan, and traceroute. Noisy. |
| `-T<0-5>` | Timing template. `-T0` = paranoid/slowest/stealthiest. `-T5` = insane/fastest, but can drop packets on unstable connections. |
| `-O` | OS detection via TCP/IP stack fingerprinting. |
| `-sV` | Service version detection. |
| `-v` / `-vv` | Verbosity — shows open ports as they're found. |

