## 2. Stealth Scans (NULL, FIN, Xmas)

These manipulate TCP flags to slip past older stateful firewalls, based on **RFC 793** behavior:
- **Closed port** → must respond with `RST`.
- **Open port** → packet is simply dropped (no response).

| Flag | Packet sent |
|---|---|
| `-sN` (NULL) | No flags set at all. |
| `-sF` (FIN) | Only `FIN` set (normally used to close a connection, not open one). |
| `-sX` (Xmas) | `PSH`, `URG`, `FIN` all set together — "lights up" like a Christmas tree in Wireshark. |

⚠️ **Caveats:**
- Modern IDS easily flags these malformed packets — not actually stealthy against current defenses.
- **Windows doesn't follow RFC 793 here** — it tends to respond with `RST` regardless of port state, so these scans will often misreport all Windows ports as closed. Don't rely on NULL/FIN/Xmas against Windows targets.

